# Cursor project rules — Breadbox (Pantry Tracker)

You are an expert mobile + TypeScript engineer working on a pantry-tracker app.
Codename "Breadbox" — the brand (name + theme) is intentionally NOT decided yet and is
decoupled from the code. Never hardcode a product name in the UI.

## What is DECIDED — build on these
- Frontend: React Native + Expo managed/prebuild workflow. Development builds are allowed;
  do not hand-write or maintain native application code.
- Language: TypeScript, strict mode. Avoid `any`; prefer precise types and interfaces.
- Validation: Zod. The canonical data model is the `PantryItem` schema in `packages/core`
  (@breadbox/core). Import it — never redefine the item shape inline.
- Architecture: offline-first. All reads/writes hit the LOCAL store first; assume the
  network may be unavailable; sync runs in the background.
- Local store + sync client: PowerSync on SQLite via `@powersync/op-sqlite` and
  `@op-engineering/op-sqlite`. PowerSync downloads automatically; the mobile connector
  uploads its CRUD queue through `services/api`.
- Backend: Node.js + TypeScript. `services/api` is a NestJS application that temporarily
  mounts three legacy Express routers while endpoints migrate incrementally to Nest modules.
- Production services: Supabase Postgres + Auth, PowerSync Cloud, and the API on Railway.
- Theming: import design tokens from `apps/mobile/src/theme/tokens.ts`. NEVER hardcode a
  color, font, or the brand name in feature code — the brand drops into that one file.

## What is OPEN — do NOT assume
- Receipt OCR vendor (Tabscanner vs Veryfi) — undecided.
- Quantity conflict semantics are NOT finished. The required design is a per-device
  contribution model with idempotent max-merge; the current production-shaped table is a
  single UUID row using `updated_at` (LWW). Do not describe the current implementation as
  sum or max-merge, and do not change the live schema without a migration/backfill plan.

## Hard rules
- Do NOT write raw native iOS (Swift/Obj-C) or Android (Java/Kotlin) code. Lean entirely on
  Expo modules. If something seems to require ejecting to a bare workflow, flag it and ask.
- Sync/merge logic must be IDEMPOTENT. Quantity merges use a per-device contribution model
  (max-merge), never a naive sum — re-syncing the same write must not change the count.
  This is a target invariant with known implementation debt; do not assume it is already met.
- Code under `spikes/` is THROWAWAY. Never import spike code into `apps/` or `packages/`,
  and don't treat it as production reference.
- Buy over build: prefer managed services (sync, auth, OCR, recipes) over hand-rolled
  infrastructure. We are a small team — spend effort on the differentiating product.
- Keep partner API keys server-side (in the backend proxy), never in the mobile bundle.

## Code style
- Functional React components with typed props (TypeScript interfaces for props).
- Atomic, scannable `.tsx` components — one clear responsibility each.
- Co-locate feature code under `apps/mobile/src/features/<feature>`.
- Prefer early returns, small functions, and explicit names over cleverness.
- ESLint + Prettier are configured at the repo root — match their output.
